Zero Flash native device backend

Implementation: original Zero source, compiled for Linux x86-64 and Windows
x86-64 by the Zero compiler. No external disk utility is launched.

zero-drives lists only disks that pass a read-only eligibility probe. Visible
numbers start at one. Each result contains the exact target path and byte size.
Administrative permissions are required to inspect raw devices. A missing
result can mean insufficient access, mounted volumes, unsupported hardware or
an unavailable hardware identity; it does not mean the computer has no disks.

Linux targets
Only canonical whole-device paths /dev/sd followed by one to three lowercase
letters are accepted. Partitions, symlinks, regular files, NVMe disks, loops
and other path aliases are rejected. The kernel must grant O_EXCL ownership
and a nonblocking exclusive flock. The descriptor must refer to a block device.
The sysfs path must pass through USB; removable must be one, ro must be zero,
partition must be absent, and the sysfs device number must match fstat rdev.
A nonempty SCSI WWID is required. Logical sectors must be 512 bytes. Capacity
comes from the opened device, not from a caller-provided size.
Mounted filesystems, active swap and claimed block devices prevent the
exclusive open. The application never unmounts them automatically.

Windows targets
Only the exact PhysicalDrive device path followed by a canonical decimal
number from zero to 255 is accepted. The storage descriptor must identify a
removable USB disk and contain a nonempty serial number. Device-number,
geometry and writable-state queries must succeed. Logical sectors must be
512 bytes. The disk containing the Windows system directory is rejected.
All volume GUIDs are enumerated, including volumes without drive letters.
Every extent must be queryable. Any drive letter or directory mount path on
the target causes refusal; no automatic dismount occurs. Writing additionally
requires an unshared physical-disk handle and successful FSCTL_LOCK_VOLUME
for every volume on that disk. Locks remain held through readback and cleanup.
A volume that cannot be classified causes refusal, even if it might belong
to another disk. This deliberately conservative case can include optical or
unusual volume drivers. USB disks without removable/serial descriptors and
USB adapters exposing fixed media are currently unsupported.

Confirmation and transfer
The front end probes first, closes the handle and asks for exact target
confirmation. A second open must match the prior hardware fingerprint,
capacity and native device number before write_allowed becomes one. The
fingerprint is an FNV-1a device identity check, not an authenticity signature.
Linux fingerprints the sysfs physical path and WWID; Windows fingerprints the
bounded storage descriptor. Transfers are 512-byte aligned and independently
checked against the opened device capacity, with an explicit byte limit per
call. Short transfers are handled; partial, zero, unaligned or failed transfers
stop the operation. The front end must flush and compare every written byte
against the immutable, SHA-256-validated image snapshot.

Backend ABI
mh points to 8192 zero-initialized bytes. Set u64 handle at offset16 to -1.
Open: offset0=1 read-only probe or2 confirmed writable;8=NUL target pointer.
Outputs:16=native handle;24=capacity;32=sector size;40=error;48=message pointer;
96=host kind;104=native identity;112=write_allowed;128=identity fingerprint.
For writable open, set136=expected fingerprint,144=expected capacity,
152=expected native identity. All three must match.
I/O:56=1 read,2 write,3 flush;64=buffer;72=byte count;80=offset;88=done.
Internal state176=Windows volume lock count,184=volume enumeration handle;
200 is reserved for the front-end coroutine dispatcher. Backend API pointers
start256; scratch buffers start512; locked-volume handles start6400.
Call close on every path, including open or transfer errors. Cleanup preserves
an earlier error. The backend uses thirteen Zero variable slots.

Verification limits
The Linux binary and Windows binary under Wine passed read-only discovery and
malformed/unavailable target rejection probes. Separate original Zero tests
exercise the exact I/O and cleanup implementation on private regular-file
handles, including write/read/flush and failure cases. Those test handles are
injected only by the separate test executable; the production opener rejects
regular files. No real USB device was erased. Physical USB writing, Windows
storage-driver behavior and hotplug behavior still require hardware validation.

Preparing an actual USB disk
On Linux, close applications using the USB disk, unmount all of its filesystems
without disconnecting the device, and ensure none of its partitions is active
swap. Start Zero Drives and Zero Flash with permission to open raw block
storage. The tool itself never disables swap or unmounts a filesystem.
On Windows, close applications using the USB disk and remove every drive-letter
and directory mount point assigned to its volumes. Run Zero Drives and Zero
Flash with administrator rights. A volume that cannot be locked remains
protected and the write is refused. Removing a drive letter is distinct from
formatting or deleting a partition; preparation does not require those actions.
After successful writing and readback, safely remove the USB device and
reconnect it before opening its new volumes or booting from it. This backend
does not force a Windows partition-layout refresh or remount old filesystems.

Final backend I/O test result
Eighteen direct I/O cases passed on Linux and Windows/Wine, plus preservation
of an existing error through cleanup. The tests compare actual 512-byte data
and 3584 untouched guard bytes in private scratch files, exercise partial EOF,
read-only write failure, alignment, capacity overflow, unconfirmed writes and
invalid-host refusal. No physical USB write is implied by these results.
